/id #uuid "e48ff996-e051-5d4b-b71b-b5bb6808f76c" :question/math-academy-id "q-168915" :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]x(t),\\,x(1)=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We have a decoupled system. Therefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial condition $x(1)=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].$ Substituting $t=1$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(1) & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3(1)}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{1} \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1}{e}^{3} \\\\ {c}_{2}e\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-3} \\\\ -{e}^{-1}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to our initial value problem is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={e}^{-3}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}-{e}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-3} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -{e}^{-1}\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167095 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "276898e7-57a4-5ca6-8df7-99fd318a2702" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Decoupled System of Linear ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "86d627ea-fc98-5e90-9321-9ba02fcb5a2e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163961} {:answer/id #uuid "a7aa757a-81d9-560a-9bb7-cc7ae32812a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163962} {:answer/id #uuid "ddcd8edd-af1c-5002-b03d-403cae009a00" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163963} {:answer/id #uuid "e3c25bcf-3448-5050-abb2-38cc402775f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163964} {:answer/id #uuid "56b92791-8adc-5a75-b183-fc76558135cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163965}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e3c25bcf-3448-5050-abb2-38cc402775f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163964} :answer-field/id #uuid "421ef6b7-af3f-503e-a607-9df5e3b3125d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163960}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ed1ca0ce-9964-570a-9175-5b218c92ebb1" :question/math-academy-id "q-321897" :question/problem "Which of the following systems of linear ODEs are decoupled?\n\n- ${x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]x(t)$\n- ${x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]x(t)$\n- ${x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]x(t)$" :question/worked-solution "A *decoupled system* consists of equations depending on only one variable, such as\n\n$$\n{x}_{i}^{′}(t)={\\lambda }_{i}{x}_{i}(t),\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\lambda }_{i}$ is a constant. This means that we can solve each equation independently. \n\nIn matrix form, a linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ is decoupled if $A$ is a diagonal matrix.\n\nWith this in mind, let's examine our systems.\n\n- Systems I and III are decoupled since their corresponding matrices are diagonal.\n\n- System II is *not* decoupled because its matrix is not diagonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167096 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "276898e7-57a4-5ca6-8df7-99fd318a2702" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Decoupled System of Linear ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e70fb0f4-70bf-538c-8ce4-a51064527073" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163967} {:answer/id #uuid "292ced5e-9d45-5913-b4e1-3e6eed8c989f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163968} {:answer/id #uuid "32e21d96-1dfd-5536-ac0a-ac6f2eb0ce3b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163969} {:answer/id #uuid "33bf0516-28e5-55bb-89bb-4f63ee421571" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163970} {:answer/id #uuid "3d9c5634-d329-524f-8201-05b10822ad63" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163971}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3d9c5634-d329-524f-8201-05b10822ad63" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163971} :answer-field/id #uuid "a97dabd6-1a88-52a3-9d11-f3403525c8c3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163966}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "93f6eb79-95de-5426-8745-3fc400e1052d" :question/math-academy-id "q-321901" :question/problem "Which of the following systems of linear ODEs are decoupled?\n\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=-3{x}_{1}(t)+2{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=-{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}$\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=-3{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=\\pi {x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}$\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)={x}_{1}(t)+2{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)={x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}$" :question/worked-solution "A *decoupled system* consists of equations depending on only one variable, such as\n\n$$\n{x}_{i}^{′}(t)={\\lambda }_{i}{x}_{i}(t),\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\lambda }_{i}$ is a constant. This means that we can solve each equation independently. \n\nWith this in mind, let's examine our systems.\n\n- Systems I is *not* decoupled because some of its equations depend on more than one variable. For example, ${x}_{1}^{′}(t)=-3{x}_{1}(t)+2{x}_{2}(t)$ depends on ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t).$\n\n- System II is decoupled since its equations depend on only one variable each. Indeed, ${x}_{1}^{′}(t)=-3{x}_{1}(t)$ depends on only ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}^{′}(t)=-{x}_{2}(t)$ depends on only ${x}_{2}(t).$\n\n- Systems III is *not* decoupled because some of its equations depend on more than one variable. For example, ${x}_{1}^{′}(t)={x}_{1}(t)+2{x}_{2}(t)$ depends on ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186353932 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "276898e7-57a4-5ca6-8df7-99fd318a2702" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Decoupled System of Linear ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a56bb6a6-7298-5f6c-b07b-8d4f41a2fdd1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353934} {:answer/id #uuid "94de02c7-3039-539e-a104-81cb024a30ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353935} {:answer/id #uuid "7e3d6d8c-858e-5ae2-8438-6020e1961207" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353936} {:answer/id #uuid "8472f07b-9650-5a8c-a1ce-334cac40df93" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353937} {:answer/id #uuid "7456163a-d835-5f26-a20b-4606dd716f99" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353938}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7456163a-d835-5f26-a20b-4606dd716f99" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353938} :answer-field/id #uuid "fa51fdba-a662-51c7-92b8-80485563989a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353933}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d2403e2c-333c-55ea-8911-693b14f580bd" :question/math-academy-id "q-331114" :question/problem "Which of the following systems of linear ODEs are decoupled?\n\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=6{x}_{1}(t)-2{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=4{x}_{1}(t)+3{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}$\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=5{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=-8{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}$\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=2{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=-{x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}$" :question/worked-solution "A *decoupled system* consists of equations depending on only one variable, such as\n\n$$\n{x}_{i}^{′}(t)={\\lambda }_{i}{x}_{i}(t),\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\lambda }_{i}$ is a constant. This means that we can solve each equation independently. \n\nWith this in mind, let's examine our systems.\n\n- System I is *not* decoupled because some of its equations depend on more than one variable. For example, ${x}_{1}^{′}(t)=6{x}_{1}(t)-2{x}_{2}(t)$ depends on ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t).$\n\n- System II is decoupled since its equations depend on only one variable each. Indeed, ${x}_{1}^{′}(t)=5{x}_{1}(t)$ depends on only ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}^{′}(t)=-8{x}_{2}(t)$ depends on only ${x}_{2}(t).$\n\n- System III is *not* decoupled because some of its equations depend on more than one variable. For example, ${x}_{1}^{′}(t)=-{x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t)$ depends on ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186353939 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "276898e7-57a4-5ca6-8df7-99fd318a2702" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Decoupled System of Linear ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b6d5b06c-ae66-5bd2-855d-1ff2c276545e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353941} {:answer/id #uuid "f4f5ac27-5338-5f97-8bef-6a6a5c2952a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353942} {:answer/id #uuid "54a6e1bf-04b1-5e93-89a1-0ea4d4fa2314" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353943} {:answer/id #uuid "b4ad393a-42ad-5517-b124-2c7c6992200a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353944} {:answer/id #uuid "a6cb62be-d7f5-5597-8ee8-7dfff8a00558" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353945}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f4f5ac27-5338-5f97-8bef-6a6a5c2952a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353942} :answer-field/id #uuid "ce956728-ef72-5e4c-b694-2160fc778aa2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353940}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6f0efb7e-837b-5d6a-a53b-848bd50fc0d4" :question/math-academy-id "q-331974" :question/problem "Which of the following systems of linear ODEs are decoupled?\n\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=3{x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=-{x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}$\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=4{x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)={x}_{1}(t)+2{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}$\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=-9{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=6{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}$" :question/worked-solution "A *decoupled system* consists of equations depending on only one variable, such as\n\n$$\n{x}_{i}^{′}(t)={\\lambda }_{i}{x}_{i}(t),\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\lambda }_{i}$ is a constant. This means that we can solve each equation independently. \n\nWith this in mind, let's examine our systems.\n\n- System I is *not* decoupled because some of its equations depend on more than one variable. For example, ${x}_{1}^{′}(t)=3{x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t)$ depends on ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t).$\n\n- System II is *not* decoupled because some of its equations depend on more than one variable. For example, ${x}_{2}^{′}(t)={x}_{1}(t)+2{x}_{2}(t)$ depends on ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t).$\n\n- System III is decoupled since its equations depend on only one variable each. Indeed, ${x}_{1}^{′}(t)=-9{x}_{1}(t)$ depends on only ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}^{′}(t)=6{x}_{2}(t)$ depends on only ${x}_{2}(t).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186353946 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "276898e7-57a4-5ca6-8df7-99fd318a2702" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Decoupled System of Linear ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cd438dcd-e746-5125-a1b0-f9281d983fb1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353948} {:answer/id #uuid "7d8eea86-bf8a-51f5-97fd-3c0fbb0e7cfe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353949} {:answer/id #uuid "fd677c28-bb09-5073-8fa8-5e0b61fc5b20" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353950} {:answer/id #uuid "98700501-137b-5147-b323-c0500b3f42a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353951} {:answer/id #uuid "de4eadd9-7559-568d-9020-83ca68b06916" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353952}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7d8eea86-bf8a-51f5-97fd-3c0fbb0e7cfe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353949} :answer-field/id #uuid "4822296e-41f5-50cf-b13d-b434713d7cd2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353947}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fec58469-5543-5101-b5c6-be6400023ba9" :question/math-academy-id "q-331976" :question/problem "Which of the following systems of linear ODEs are decoupled?\n\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=-12{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=5{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}$\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=2{x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=5{x}_{1}(t)-3{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}$\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)={x}_{1}(t)+6{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)={x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}$" :question/worked-solution "A *decoupled system* consists of equations depending on only one variable, such as\n\n$$\n{x}_{i}^{′}(t)={\\lambda }_{i}{x}_{i}(t),\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\lambda }_{i}$ is a constant. This means that we can solve each equation independently. \n\nWith this in mind, let's examine our systems.\n\n- System I is decoupled since its equations depend on only one variable each. Indeed, ${x}_{1}^{′}(t)=-12{x}_{1}(t)$ depends on only ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}^{′}(t)=5{x}_{2}(t)$ depends on only ${x}_{2}(t).$\n\n- System II is *not* decoupled because some of its equations depend on more than one variable. For example, ${x}_{1}^{′}(t)=2{x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t)$ depends on ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t).$\n\n- System III is *not* decoupled because some of its equations depend on more than one variable. For example, ${x}_{1}^{′}(t)={x}_{1}(t)+6{x}_{2}(t)$ depends on ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186353953 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ce35e5a9-2444-5e5f-9463-5a4d54496d9d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution to a Decoupled Linear System of Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6c2b7748-da67-5f7c-9878-11344b043916" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t},\\,{c}_{1}\\in R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353955} {:answer/id #uuid "3ca13edd-2412-5791-993a-bb4aa8e60ebb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{10t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353956} {:answer/id #uuid "c4a6af28-1b6c-53cd-9187-5127fbca8bba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{10t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353957} {:answer/id #uuid "5b9986e0-fc75-53ef-a617-1817c60a94f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t},\\,{c}_{1}\\in R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353958} {:answer/id #uuid "c1957d01-8a50-5324-81eb-a198b95b4174" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{5t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353959}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3ca13edd-2412-5791-993a-bb4aa8e60ebb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{10t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353956} :answer-field/id #uuid "b2e7f564-e143-586e-bd69-51346dab0d46" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353954}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1325a9dc-07b0-53c3-a6ca-3ecc06b6fe95" :question/math-academy-id "q-331989" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the matrix differential equation $\\,{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 10\\end{aligned}]x(t).$" :question/worked-solution "Given a decoupled system of first-order linear ODEs in the form\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {\\lambda }_{2}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe formula for the general solution is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSince the matrix of our differential equation is diagonal, the system is decoupled. \n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{10t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186353960 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ce35e5a9-2444-5e5f-9463-5a4d54496d9d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution to a Decoupled Linear System of Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c5bafbc8-b733-5d8a-bcf6-d414ce6245d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-5t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353962} {:answer/id #uuid "7b4ebf76-2ed3-5b1e-932a-7bf5e43605c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t},\\,{c}_{1}\\in R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353963} {:answer/id #uuid "655af992-dd3c-5469-8025-461cc357d5eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={c}